1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 c5 3. d5 e6 4. Nc3 exd5 5. cxd5 d6 6. Nf3 g6 7. Nd2 Nbd7 8. e4 Bg7 9. Be2 O-O 10. O-O Re8 11. a4 g5 $5 {"This unlikely looking move is not the result of home cooking. Ray gets all sorts of Soviet chess magazines from a Russian bookshop in Charing Cross Road and from one of these he extracted analysis on this surprising pawn push." (Jimmy Adams, CHESS Magazine)} 12. Ra3 (12. Nc4 Nxe4 13. Nxe4 Rxe4 14. Nxd6 Rd4 {, Bonchev-Mi.Tseitlin, Bulgaroa 1977, was the game in the Russian chess magazine.}) 12... Ne5 13. Kh1 Ng6 14. Bb5 Re7 15. Re1 Ng4 16. Rf1 ({"The grandmaster's second choice. First of all Petrosian had his hand only millimetres away from the f-pawn – and he wasn't joking either. He was actually going to play} 16. f3 {. Then at the last moment he pulled his hand away and staggered back as he realised what he had nearly done." (Adams)}) 16... h6 17. Be2 N4e5 18. Qe1 a6 19. Nd1 Bd7 20. a5 Nf4 21. Ne3 f5 22. exf5 $2 Nxe2 23. Qxe2 Bb5 24. Qd1 Bxf1 25. Qxf1 c4 $1 26. Ndxc4 Nxc4 27. Nxc4 Rc8 28. Be3 Re4 29. b3 Qf6 30. h3 g4 31. hxg4 Rxg4 32. Kg1 Kh7 33. Qd3 Rh4 34. Ra2 Rg8 35. Rd2 Rh5 36. Rd1 Bf8 37. Qe4 Qxf5 38. Qxf5+ Rxf5 39. Kf1 Rg4 40. f3 Rg6 41. Kf2 Be7 42. g3 h5 43. Rh1 Rxd5 {"A case of biter bit; Black prepared the move 11...g5 from an article which appeared in '64' while Petrosian was its editor" (Source: BCM, May 1978, p215; CHESS, April 1978, Vol.43/791-2, p214)} 0-1

